---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Status from "../../components/Status.astro";
import { REPO, OHF, data } from "../../lib/data";
---
<Base title="How we get the data, and what we do not guarantee" current="/about/">
  <header class="head"><h1>How we get the data, and what we do not guarantee</h1></header>
  <div class="prose sec">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Home Assistant core</strong> — <a href="https://github.com/home-assistant/core/blob/dev/homeassistant/generated/integrations.json">integrations.json</a>
        and each integration's <code>manifest.json</code> (Apache-2.0): the name, its <code>iot_class</code> (local_polling / local_push = local; cloud_* = cloud)
        and whether it has settable entity platforms (<code>number</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>switch</code>, <code>button</code>). Core models are not listed:
        the <a href={OHF}>Open Home Foundation Device Database</a> does that from real installations, and we link to it.</li>
      <li><strong>HACS custom integrations</strong> — picked by hand from the <a href="https://hacs.xyz/">HACS</a> default store (energy devices only; generic
        protocol bridges such as OCPP or SunSpec and automation helpers are left out). Stars and last update come from the HACS store data, <code>iot_class</code>
        from the repo's manifest, the platforms from the repo itself. What a repo covers is written from its README, in its own words.</li>
      <li><strong>evcc</strong> — the device templates in <a href="https://github.com/evcc-io/evcc/tree/master/templates/definition">templates/definition</a> (MIT):
        brand, product, connection parameters and capabilities. Templates that need an <a href="https://docs.evcc.io/en/docs/sponsorship">evcc sponsor token</a>
        are marked; no template content is copied, only the fact of support.</li>
      <li><strong>Grid registers</strong> — the published type-approval lists of {Object.keys(data.registers).length} registers (see <a href="/grid/">Grid lists</a>).
        Device pages show every list row for the matched models. The <a href="/grid/">grid lists view</a> also shows each row's status word, firmware,
        row or document number and dates — the registers' facts, not GridFit's work, and not under GridFit's CC BY. None of the lists states reuse terms; every row links back,
        and the register is the authority. Belgium's Synergrid list allows personal use only, so the cross-view shows just “listed” / “expired” with a link, and family pages do not use it.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>What the marks mean</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><Status kind="local" /> the integration or template talks to the device (or its logger) on your network. It says nothing about whether the device <em>also</em> needs the internet for setup or updates.
        <Status kind="cloud" /> it goes through the vendor's cloud. <Status kind="none" /> no integration found — not “does not work”.</li>
      <li><Status kind="write" /> Home Assistant: the integration has entities you can set — anything from a battery charge limit to a restart button; read its docs for what exactly.
        evcc: chargers are controlled by definition; meters only when the template has <code>battery-control</code>. <Status kind="read" /> sensors only.</li>
      <li><strong>Grid cells, one answer per model and country.</strong> In a country whose list we read: <span class="cl cl-yes">✓</span> on the list (the hover says whether with a condition or end date), <span class="cl cl-no">✕</span> refused or expired, empty = not on the list — the match is a model-code pattern, not a confirmation; each model shows how its brand name was matched (exact / alias). In a country without a list, or whose list we have not read: <span class="cl cl-doc">✓</span> the maker's own released-countries or certificate document names the country (<code>data/evidence/</code>; the grid operator decides); <span class="cl cl-else">○</span> accepted on other EU lists — the maker has had the model certified and other grid operators accept it, nothing is checked in that country itself; <span class="cl cl-type">B</span> over the country's type-A ceiling (a country profile states it), type B being a procedure of its own; <span class="cl cl-free">—</span> nothing to check for this class (a wallbox is a load, only Austria lists chargers; a meter is not a grid-connection unit anywhere); <strong>?</strong> not confirmed; empty = nothing known (its only listings are expired or refused). Source of the per-country states: the EU+ grid-connection table of 2026-10-29 and the country profiles.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>What we do not guarantee</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>An empty cell is not a “no”.</strong> We never say a device does not work or is not approved — only what a source says. “Accepted on other EU lists” is a hint about the maker, not an approval in your country.</li>
      <li><strong>Counts add up.</strong> Every filter in the grid view is a partition: the “Any” count equals the sum of its options, and a missing value is its own option (“not specified”, “not collected”, “no MPPT”) rather than a silent gap. A script proves it on every build (<code>web/test/facets.mjs</code>).</li>
      <li><strong>Firmware changes things.</strong> Vendors have closed local APIs with an update before. Every row shows the date we last saw it.</li>
      <li><strong>Grid rules are yours to check.</strong> Changing export limits or charging a battery from the grid can break your connection agreement. Ask your installer or grid operator.</li>
      <li>Nothing here is a purchase recommendation, and no manufacturer pays for a listing.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>The dataset lives in a public <a href={REPO}>GitHub repository</a> (data CC BY 4.0, code MIT). Open an issue or a pull request —
      “my Deye does Modbus TCP from firmware X” is exactly what we need. The site rebuilds from the sources weekly; <a href="/changes/">Changes</a> lists what moved.</p>
    <h2>Trademarks</h2>
    <p>Home Assistant is a trademark of the Open Home Foundation; evcc, HACS and all product and company names are trademarks of their owners,
      used here only to say what works with what. This site is not affiliated with any of them.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
